  "vulnerability_information": "# Stored XSS in the Classic block element path\n\n**Affected:** WordPress development build `7.2-alpha-64254`, commit [`7fdd5ebe4bbcf9e92d7f85ae662efabc936f4235`](https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/commit/7fdd5ebe4bbcf9e92d7f85ae662efabc936f4235). No released version contained this change as of 2026-10-09; stock 7.1.3 retains `elementpath = false`.\n\n**Summary:** A default Author can save an image with an entity-encoded HTML payload in `data-wp-more`. WordPress KSES preserves the `data-*` attribute. When an Administrator edits the post in the Classic block and selects the image, the WordPress TinyMCE plugin copies its decoded attribute value into `ResolveName`. TinyMCE's modern theme inserts that name into the element-path UI as HTML. The development commit removed the editor setting that previously disabled this path, enabling stored script execution in the Administrator's browser. The relevant code is [`class-wp-editor.php`](https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/7fdd5ebe4bbcf9e92d7f85ae662efabc936f4235/wp-includes/class-wp-editor.php#L937-L945), the WordPress TinyMCE [`ResolveName` handler](https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/7fdd5ebe4bbcf9e92d7f85ae662efabc936f4235/wp-includes/js/tinymce/plugins/wordpress/plugin.js#L195-L202), and the modern theme [element-path renderer](https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/7fdd5ebe4bbcf9e92d7f85ae662efabc936f4235/wp-includes/js/tinymce/themes/modern/theme.js#L5807-L5818).\n\n## Minimal reproduction\n1. From this directory, run `cp .env.example .env` and `docker compose up -d --build`, open `http://127.0.0.1:18081`, complete WordPress installation, and create a user with the **Author** role. The Dockerfile pins the affected development commit and includes its compiled editor assets.\n2. Sign in as the Author. In **Posts → Add New**, open the top-right **⋮ → Code editor** and enter a title and this content:\n\n   ```html\n   <p><img src=\"/wp-includes/images/w-logo-blue-white-bg.png\" width=\"100\" data-wp-more=\"&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(document.domain)&gt;\"></p>\n   ```\n{F6948286}\n\n3. Switch to **Visual editor** and save the draft. The content becomes a Classic (`core/freeform`) block, with the encoded attribute intact.\n4. Sign in as an Administrator and edit that draft. Select the Classic block, click **Edit**, then click the image inside the Classic Editor modal.\n\n{F6948287}\n\n**Observed result:** The image's `data-wp-more` value is decoded and inserted into the element-path HTML. The nested image fails to load and `alert(document.domain)` runs in the Administrator's editor page. This requires the Administrator to open the Classic editor and select the image; merely viewing the post does not trigger it.\n\n## Impact\n\nA WordPress author is able to create a malicious post. If an admin select the image in the post in the classic block editor, then an attacker-chosen XSS payload executes.",
